  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 335 PM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Isolated storms across eastern NC possible this \nafternoon/evening.  \n\nIsolated thunderstorms are expected across portions of Eastern NC \nthis afternoon, mainly along and east of the I-95 corridor, as \ndaytime heating interacts with the sea breeze and a lingering \nboundary. Latest ensemble guidance indicates storms will develop \nalong the sea breeze with limited inland progression into the \nCoastal Plain, though a few isolated strong storms capable of gusty \nwinds cannot be ruled out. Storm activity will rapidly diminish \nafter sunset. Overnight, areas that received rainfall may see low \nstratus clouds and patchy fog develop where winds become light and \ncalm.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A slight risk (2 of 5) of severe weather on \nTuesday. The main hazard will be damaging winds, with large hail and \na tornado or two also possible. \n\nDetails: Large-scale ascent associated with an approaching mid-level \nshortwave trough and an attendant cold front will provide sufficient \nforcing for convective initiation Tuesday afternoon, with storms \nbecoming more widespread through the evening. Ahead of the front, \nsoutherly winds will bring warm and moist air to the region, \nsupporting MUCAPE of roughly 1500-3000 J/kg Tuesday afternoon. This \nwill combine with 40-50 kts of 0-8 km shear to support initially \nsupercells with a localized wind and hail threat. Storm interactions \nin the late afternoon and evening should then favor clustered \nconvection and bowing segments, with the primary hazard being a \ndamaging wind threat. A brief tornado or two will also be possible \nas the low-level jet strengthens and increases low-level shear and \nhelicity from the Triangle eastward. The threat of flooding should \nbe a lesser threat due to quicker storm propagations expected. High \nrain rates within the strongest storms may produce localized \nflooding in urban and poor drainage areas. \n\nTiming: Storms look to start forming and approach the region in the \nearly afternoon, with storms entering the western Piedmont around 2 \nto 4 PM before progressing eastward across the region. The most \nlikely timing for the severe weather threat currently appears to be \nbetween 4 and 10 PM, potentially lasting until midnight.\n\nWhat could diminish the threat: While confidence is increasing in \nthe threat of severe weather, the primary forecast uncertainty \nremains how upstream convection currently located in the Great Lakes \nregion will evolve overnight and into tomorrow. If rain/cloud cover \nis able to persist over the mountains into the morning hours more \nthan currently expected, the destabilization in the Carolinas would \nbe reduced tomorrow afternoon, leading to a lesser severe threat.",
